nt 3's public message. Now it's Agent 1's turn. We need to respond to the discussion, referencing prior points, and give our current recommendation. We have to consider the shared mental model: currently all agents are voting Candidate B. We need to either maintain or change. We should provide argument, maybe bring up other candidate facts not yet discussed, or ask for missing info. But we must not ask new tool calls now; we can ask in next turn if needed. We need to produce a focused argumentative contribution.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate A anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather conditions, excellent computer skills, can be grumpy, can be uncooperative. Also from other agents: weak memory for numbers, nasty remarks, pretentious. Candidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, regarded as egocentric, not very willing to further his education, 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere with crew. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as arrogant, relatively weak leadership skills.\n\nWe need to discuss and maybe bring up a point about another candidate to compare. Perhaps we can argue that Candidate C's reliability and crew atmosphere are strong, but his egocentric and lack of willingness to develop are concerns. Or we could argue that Candidate D's responsibility and problem solving are strong, but arrogance and weak leadership are concerns. Or we could argue that Candidate A's anticipation of danger and leadership are strong, but unorganized and not good at criticism are concerns.\n\nWe need to decide whether to keep B or shift. The panel seems leaning B. We could keep B, but we should bring up a new perspective: maybe highlight that Candidate C's quick correct decisions and 100% reliability could be a strong alternative, but his egocentric nature may affect crew cooperation. Or we could ask for more info on B's adaptability, but we already know it's missing. Maybe we can suggest that we need to assess B's adaptability before final decision.\n\nThus our contribution: we can say we still support B, but we need to address the missing adaptability info, and also compare with C's reliability and crew atmosphere. Provide explicit facts: C 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, but difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education. So B still seems best overall.\n\nThus public message: \"I agree with Agent 3's recommendation for B, but note that we still lack data on adaptability. However, compared to C's egocentric and lack of development, D's arrogance, and A's unorganized nature, B's strengths outweigh his concerns.\"\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress well, good weather assessment, excellent computer skills; C 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, but difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education; D takes responsibility seriously, solves problems extremely well, but arrogant, weak leadership; A anticipates dangerous situations, leadership, but unorganized, not good at criticism.\n\nThus we state vote B.\n\nNow produce output."}
